{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_014DjRp51pw96RYV7J2P6mCa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see the actual state clearly:\n- Items 1-13 and 15: DONE\n- Item 14: TODO (Security integration tests) - P0, S\n- Items 16-19: TODO\n\nItem 13 is already DONE (it was in the restore commit). My work to create EmployeeValidatorTest.java was already done by a prior session. The current TODO items are:\n1. Item 14 (P0, S) - Security integration tests\n2. Item 16 (P1, M) - EmployeeController\n3. Items 17-19 (P1, M) - Controller integration tests\n\n\nI'm starting with Item 14 since it's the highest priority. The spec requires JUnit 4 integration tests for SecurityConfig using Spring MockMvc and spring-security-test, covering unauthenticated access returning 401, user GET permissions, user POST restrictions with 403, admin POST permissions, and JSON ErrorResponse formatting for error codes. But I'm realizing there might be a complication with the test setup...\n\nSpring Security's filter chain runs before request routing, so unauthenticated requests get intercepted with 401 and authenticated users making unauthorized POST requests get 403 before the controller is even reached. For the user GET test, if the EmployeeController doesn't exist yet, Spring Security would allow the request through but then routing would fail with 404—though the security aspect of the test (verifying Spring Security permits the request) would still be valid. I should probably mock or stub the controller endpoint to avoid the 404 issue and keep the focus on security behavior. , I think the cleanest approach is to set up a WebApplicationContext with Spring Security and a stub controller so I'm actually testing the security filter chain rather than just mocking around it.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the existing SecurityConfig to understand how it's structured.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage"